One dictionary from id to tracked instance is the identity map:

```csharp
public Customer? Find(int id) => _identityMap.TryGetValue(id, out var tracked) ? tracked : Query(id);

public Customer? Query(int id)
{
    var row = db.Select(id);
    if (row is null) return null;
    if (_identityMap.TryGetValue(id, out var tracked)) return tracked;   // keep local edits
    var entity = Materialise(id, row);
    _identityMap[id] = entity;
    return entity;
}
```

The trap test catches the plausible wrong version that refreshes the tracked instance with the row it just read: that silently throws away the pending edit, and it is not what EF Core does (a tracking query never overwrites a tracked entity's values; reload explicitly when you need fresh data). A `Find` that always queries fails the one-query tests, and caching a miss as "not found" fails the boundary test.
